  "query_preview": "..The first day of October will usher in quiet\nweather conditions for all of Southeast Arizona. The upper \npattern this morning is defined by the departing upper-low \nmoving NE through the Oklahoma panhandle and an amplified ridge \nof high pressure off the California and Baja MX coastlines. \n\nThis upper ridge is expected to strengthen as it builds into \nthe Great Basin/Desert Southwest into this weekend. As this \noccurs, it will force a surface high to dive south along the \nsouthern Rockies today and back into Southeast Arizona as a \nbackdoor front Friday into early next week. This will bring\nabout breezy to locally windy southeast to east winds to the \nmajority of the area. The latest 01/06Z NBM 1-D Viewer indicates\nthe strongest winds will generally occur Sunday and Monday. The \nprobability mean for maximum wind gusts for the Tucson International \nAirport (KTUS) on FRI/SAT/SUN/MON as 42kts/37kts/41kts/36kts.\nDust shouldn't be a problem with this feature given the saturated\nsoils. \n\nOtherwise, afternoon high temperatures will warm to near normal\nwest of Tucson by this weekend, but areas east of Tucson will\nstill remain below normal due to the lingering backdoor front.\nAdditional building of the mean ridge the middle of next week\nand the waning frontal influence will result in above normal \ntemperatures across all of Southeast Arizona starting next\nWednesday.",
